{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790540225,"nonce":"oxX0LoXAZhxChzGE+7iha9NSVrw=","message":"Step 2, browser: memfs mounts a real directory where the browser has one. A page mounts a FileSystemDirectoryHandle at a path under memfs: showDirectoryPicker where the browser has it (Chromium), or OPFS (navigator.storage.getDirectory()) in every current browser. Directory access in the browser is async only, and FS calls are synchronous, so the directory is loaded into memfs before the program starts, and memfs stays the program's view. Writes, mkdirs, removes and renames under a mount go into a change log; the page drains it (fs_take_changes) and applies it to the handle in the background, so the program sees its own writes at once and the directory catches up. Changes made to the directory from outside the page appear at the next mount. The embedded crate exports fs_mount(path) and fs_take_changes(); wlift.js does the directory walk and the write-back. Once fibers can suspend in wasm (step 4), a read could wait on the directory directly instead of the whole tree being loaded up front.","files":null}]}